WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:09.310
In this week, you hear about sequence-to-sequence models, which are useful for everything from

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:11.750
machine translation to speech recognition.

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:16.590
Let's start with the basic models, and then later this week, you hear about beam search,

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:21.790
the attention model, and we'll wrap up with discussion of models for audio data like speech.

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:24.990
Let's get started.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:31.670
Let's say you want to input a French sentence like Jean-Vissic Lefrique in September, and

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:37.350
you want to translate it to the English sentence, Jean is visiting Africa in September.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:44.110
As usual, let's use X1 through X, in this case 5, to represent the words in the input

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:51.470
sequence, and we'll use Y1 through Y6 to represent the words in the output sequence.

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:58.230
So how can you train a neural network to input the sequence X and output the sequence Y?

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:04.430
Well, here's something you could do, and the ideas I'm about to present are mainly from

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:13.150
these two papers, due to Iliosasquivel, Oyovaniels, and Quokkular, and that one by Qiang-Hyun

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:20.550
Cho, Bat Van, Marion Boer, Kaggler, Gausser, Dimitri, Badenio, Feghi Booger, Hulger Strank,

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:21.950
and Jocelyn Jo.

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:29.030
First, let's have a network, which we're going to call the encoder network, be built

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:36.670
as a RNN, and this could be a GRU or an LSTM, feeding the input French words one word at

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:37.990
a time.

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:46.630
And after ingesting the input sequence, the RNN then outputs a vector that represents the

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:48.550
input sentence.

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:54.270
After that, you can build a decoder network, which I'm going to draw here, which takes

00:01:54.270 --> 00:02:01.430
its encoding output by the encoder network, shown in black on the left, and then can

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:11.190
be trained to output the translation one word at a time.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:18.350
And so eventually it outputs the, say, the end of sequence or end of sentence token upon

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:19.910
which the decoder stops.

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:27.350
And as usual, we could take the generated tokens and feed them to the next set of hidden

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:31.870
states in the sequence, like we were doing before when synthesizing text using the language

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:33.470
model.

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:38.870
One of the most remarkable recent results in deep learning is that this model works.

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:46.750
In enough pairs of French and English sentences, if you train a model to input a French sentence

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:52.830
and output the corresponding English translation, this will actually work decently well.

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:58.710
And this model simply uses an encoding network whose job it is to find an encoding of the

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:05.110
input French sentence and then use a decoder network to then generate the corresponding

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:07.670
English translation.

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:14.110
And architecture very similar to this also works for image captioning.

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:19.110
So given an image like the one shown here, maybe you want it to be captioned automatically

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:22.010
as a cat sitting on a chair.

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:29.590
So how do you train a neural network to input an image and output a caption like that phrase

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:30.590
up there?

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:33.790
Here's what you can do.

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:40.070
From the earlier course on Confnet, you've seen how you can input an image into a convolutional

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:47.430
network, maybe a pre-trained AlexNet, and have that learn and encoding, learn a set of features

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:49.050
of the input image.

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:52.870
So this is actually the AlexNet architecture.

00:03:52.870 --> 00:04:00.950
And if we get rid of this final softmax unit, the pre-trained AlexNet can give you a 4096-dimensional

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:05.950
feature vector with which to represent this picture of a cat.

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:11.430
And so this pre-trained network can be the encoder network for the image.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:17.910
And you now have a 4000 or 4096-dimensional vector that represents the image.

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:26.830
You can then take this and feed it to an RNN whose job it is to generate the caption

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:29.390
one word at a time.

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:37.070
So similar to what we saw with machine translation, translating from French to English, you can

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:47.790
now input a feature vector describing the input and then have it generate an output sequence

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:51.190
or output set of words one word at a time.

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:56.270
And this actually works pretty well for image captioning, especially if the caption you want

00:04:56.270 --> 00:04:59.310
to generate is not too long.

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:07.070
As far as I know, this type of model was first proposed by Junhuan Mao, Weishu Yiyang, Jiang

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:09.710
Wang, Jihan Huang, and Alan Yiu.

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:13.670
Although it turns out there were multiple groups coming up with very similar models

00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:19.090
independently and at about the same time.

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:24.330
So two other groups that had done very similar work at about the same time and I think independently

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:30.050
of Mao Dao were Olyubinyeos, Alexander Tochev, Sammy Bengio, and Dimitru Erhan, as well

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:33.730
as Andre Kapathy and Fei-Fei Yi.

00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:39.230
So you've now seen how a basic sequence to sequence model works, how a basic image to

00:05:39.230 --> 00:05:42.210
sequence or image captioning model works.

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:46.570
But there are some differences between how you would run a model like this to generate

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:53.330
a sequence compared to how you were synthesizing novel text using a language model.

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:57.730
One of the key differences is you don't want a randomly chosen translation, you maybe

00:05:57.730 --> 00:06:02.410
want the most likely translation, or you don't want a randomly chosen caption, maybe

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:06.570
not, but you might want the best caption, the most likely caption.

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:10.210
So let's see in the next video how you go about generating that.
